Q: error: expected specifier-qualifier-list before ‘VarShapeFunc’ A: <p>As already written in the answer of <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/589924/ikegami">ikegami</a> in these  lines</p>
<pre><code>        VarShapeFunc* shape;
        VarFunc* forward;
        VarFunc* backward;
</code></pre>
<p>of the structure declaration</p>
<pre><code>struct VarType {
        const char* name;
        u32 num_inputs;
        VarShapeFunc* shape;
        VarFunc* forward;
        VarFunc* backward;
};
</code></pre>
<p>there are used function names of the declared functions <strong>VarShapeFunc</strong> and <strong>VarFunc</strong> instead of type specifiers in the pointer declarations.</p>
<p>I would like to show one more possibility to declare the pointers if your compiler supports the C23 Standard. You could use the <strong>typeof</strong> specifier the following way:</p>
<pre><code>struct VarType {
        const char* name;
        u32 num_inputs;
        typeof( VarShapeFunc ) *shape;
        typeof( VarFunc ) *forward;
        typeof( VarFunc ) *backward;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Here is a simple demonstration program</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

static void f( const char *s )
{
    puts( s );
}

int main( void )
{
    struct A
    {
        typeof( f ) *f;
    } a = { f };

    a.f( &quot;Hello C23 Standard and typeof specifier!&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Its output is</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Hello C23 Standard and typeof specifier!
</code></pre>
